      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "content": {
          "kind": "markdown",
          "value": "Absolutely. I'd add these as **comments/notes rather than rewriting the existing graph**, because most of what's there is already right.\n\n> **Separate Event from Occurrence**\n>\n> An Event is a declaration of something that can happen. An Occurrence is one specific instance of that Event having happened.\n>\n> This mirrors the Method/Invocation distinction:\n>\n> `Method → Invocation`\n>\n> `Event → Occurrence`\n>\n> The Event declares name, payload contract, valid source semantics, visibility, and other policy. The Occurrence carries the actual source, payload, identity, time, and runtime context.\n\n> **Occurrence identity**\n>\n> Each occurrence should have its own identity independent of the Event definition.\n>\n> Occurrence identity gives Twilite a stable object for tracing, deduplication, ordering, correlation, replay, debugging, and delivery bookkeeping.\n>\n> Persistence of occurrences is a separate policy decision. First-class runtime identity does not imply that every occurrence must become a persistent graph node.\n\n> **Separate Occurrence from Delivery**\n>\n> An Event occurs once regardless of how many subscribers exist.\n>\n> If one occurrence has five subscriptions, that is:\n>\n> `1 occurrence → 5 deliveries`\n>\n> Each delivery may independently succeed, fail, be filtered, delayed, cancelled, or potentially retried.\n>\n> Delivery failure must not alter the fact that the original occurrence happened.\n\n> **Subscription is declarative**\n>\n> A Subscription declares interest in an Event and identifies what should receive matching occurrences.\n>\n> Prefer explicit graph relationships for subscriptions rather than hiding subscriptions inside runtime callback registration.\n>\n> Conceptually:\n>\n> `Event → Subscription → Handler`\n>\n> Runtime delivery follows that declared relationship.\n\n> **Events report; Methods command**\n>\n> Preserve a hard semantic boundary:\n>\n> **Events report that something happened. Methods request that something be done.**\n>\n> An Event must not silently become a command.\n>\n> A handler receiving an occurrence may choose to invoke a Method, but that is a separate action with its own Invocation, authority checks, lifecycle, effects, and outcome.\n>\n> Conceptually:\n>\n> `Occurrence → Delivery → Handler → Method Invocation`\n\n> **Causality and correlation**\n>\n> Occurrences and Method Invocations should leave room for causal/correlation information.\n>\n> Example:\n>\n> `Invocation A`\n>\n> causes a Property change,\n>\n> which produces `Occurrence B`,\n>\n> whose delivery invokes `Invocation C`,\n>\n> which produces `Occurrence D`.\n>\n> Twilite should eventually be capable of reconstructing that chain and answering **\"Why did this happen?\"**\n>\n> The initial implementation does not need a complete tracing system. It only needs to avoid designing away the ability to represent causality later.\n\n> **Source versus cause**\n>\n> The source of an Event and the cause of an Occurrence are not necessarily the same thing.\n>\n> A Property may be the source of `valueChanged`, while a particular Method Invocation may be the reason that Property changed.\n>\n> Preserve this distinction:\n>\n> `source = where the occurrence originated`\n>\n> `cause = what led to this occurrence`\n\n> **Payload belongs to the Occurrence**\n>\n> The Event declares the payload contract. The Occurrence contains the actual payload.\n>\n> Therefore:\n>\n> `Event.payloadContract ≠ Occurrence.payload`\n>\n> This parallels Properties and Methods: declarations describe what is allowed; runtime objects represent what actually happened.\n\nAnd I'd add one final higher-level comment because I think you've now earned it:\n\n> **Scripting primitive symmetry**\n>\n> The scripting model is converging on three declaration/runtime pairs:\n>\n> `Property → Resolution → Effective Value`\n>\n> `Method → Invocation → Outcome / Effects`\n>\n> `Event → Occurrence → Delivery / Reaction`\n>\n> Preserve this symmetry where it is natural, but do not force the three systems to become structurally identical. Each primitive describes a different semantic category: **state, action, and occurrence**.\n\nThat last bit is important. I'd actually put it on the parent **Twilite Scripting System** graph eventually, rather than only Events.\n\nBecause I think you've found the spine of the whole thing now:\n\n**State. Action. Occurrence.**\n\nProperties, Methods, Events.\n\nThat's wonderfully small for how much machinery it can generate. ⚡\n"
        },
        "interfaceContract": {
          "version": 1,
          "receivesViewContent": true
        },
